  "moduleId": "m00221",
  "title": {
    "segmentId": "m00221:title:auto-1",
    "text": "Nucleophilic Addition of Alcohols: Acetal Formation"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "5f62661c-475c-4de4-915d-94da328b9289"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00221:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00001",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00221:alt:fig-00001-alt",
          "text": "Cyclohexanone reacts with two equivalents of methanol and hydrochloric acid catalyst to form cyclohexanone dimethyl acetal and water."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_19_10_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00002",
      "segmentId": "m00221:para:para-00002"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00002",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00221:alt:fig-00002-alt",
          "text": "The structure shows a reversible reaction where a carbonyl group is protonated at oxygen by H A; resonance produces neutral O H group single bonded to carbocation."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_19_10_002.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00003",
      "segmentId": "m00221:para:para-00003"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00003",
      "class": "mechanism-figure scaled-down",
      "caption": {
        "segmentId": "m00221:caption:fig-00003-caption"
      },
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00221:alt:fig-00003-alt",
          "text": "Ketone or aldehyde to acetal via seven steps: protonation of O, alcohol attacks carbonyl, R O H deprotonated to hemiacetal, protonation of hydroxy, loss of water, alcohol attacks oxonium, deprotonation."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_19_10_003.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00004",
      "segmentId": "m00221:para:para-00004"
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00005",
      "segmentId": "m00221:para:para-00005"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00004",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00221:alt:fig-00004-alt",
          "text": "Ethyl 4-oxopentanoate reacts with an unknown reagent represented as a question mark to produce 5-hydroxy-2-pentanone."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_19_10_004.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00006",
      "segmentId": "m00221:para:para-00006"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00005",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00221:alt:fig-00005-alt",
          "text": "Ethyl 4-oxopentanoate reacts with glycol and acid to form acetal and water. This reacts with lithium aluminum hydride to form another product. This reacts with hydronium ion to form 5-hydroxy-2-pentanone."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_19_10_005.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00007",
      "segmentId": "m00221:para:para-00007"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00006",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00221:alt:fig-00006-alt",
          "text": "The figure shows two interconvertable forms of glucose. The glucose-open chain transforms into glucose-cyclic hemiacetal."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_19_10_006.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
